        "content": "{\n  \"rule\": \"Return this JSON object with the rule field unchanged and a new passage of about 250 words in the text field.\",\n  \"text\": \"The laundrette on Bell Street returned something extra with every wash.\\n\\nUsually a sock. Sometimes a handkerchief embroidered with initials nobody recognised.\\n\\nThen I found my mother’s cardigan.\\n\\nWe had buried her in it.\\n\\nI knew the missing button, the darn beneath one sleeve. It was still warm.\\n\\nThe attendant watched me hold it against my chest.\\n\\n“Lost property,” he said.\\n\\n“She wasn’t lost.”\\n\\nHe glanced at the machines.\\n\\n“Everyone says that.”\\n\\nI took it home. That evening, the kitchen smelled of her cigarettes. By morning, someone had peeled the potatoes I’d left beside the sink.\\n\\nI went back with a bag of washing and the cardigan.\\n\\nThe attendant shook his head.\\n\\n“No returns.”\\n\\n“I want to know where it came from.”\\n\\nHe pointed to machine nine.\\n\\nIts drum was empty. Behind the round glass, something tapped twice.\\n\\nMy mother’s knock.\\n\\nI put my hand on the door.\\n\\nThe attendant caught my wrist.\\n\\n“Wait until the cycle finishes.”\\n\\nThe display read eleven minutes.\\n\\nI sat down.\\n\\nAt closing time, it still read eleven minutes.\\n\\nThrough the glass, a pale hand pressed against mine. Her wedding ring clicked on the drum.\\n\\n“Can you hear me?” I whispered.\\n\\nThe hand turned over.\\n\\nAcross its palm, in blue ink, she had written a word.\\n\\nRUN.\\n\\nBehind me, the attendant locked the street door.\\n\\nEvery machine stopped at once.\\n\\nHe wheeled an empty laundry basket toward my chair.\\n\\n“She always spoils it,” he said.\\n\\nThe hand withdrew. Something much larger shifted inside the drum.\\n\\nThe display changed to END.\\n\\nThen the door clicked open.\"\n}",
